    symbol: '🛡️',
    powers: ['Pedang Divin', 'Kesetiaan', 'Ketangkasan', 'Perlindungan', 'Keberanian'],
    powersEn: ['Divine Swordsmanship', 'Loyalty', 'Agility', 'Protection', 'Courage'],
    story: '''
Lakshmana adalah putra Raja Dasharatha dari Ayodhya bersama istrinya Queen Sumitra, dan merupakan adik kembar dari Shatrughna. Ia secara spiritual dianggap sebagai penjelmaan Shesha Naga, ular kosmis tempat Dewa Wisnu berbaring, yang menunjukkan hubungan tak terpisahkan antara Lakshmana dan Rama — di mana Wisnu (Rama) berada, di sana pula Shesha (Lakshmana) akan berada. Sejak kecil, Lakshmana telah terikat secara mendalam dengan Rama, lebih menghormati Rama daripada ayahnya sendiri, dan ketika Rama diasingkan ke hutan selama empat belas tahun, Lakshmana memutuskan untuk ikut tanpa ragu sedikit pun, meninggalkan istrinya Urmila di Ayodhya.

Selama pengasingan, Lakshmana berperan sebagai pelindung utama Rama dan Sita, menjaga pondok mereka di hutan dengan kewaspadaan yang tak pernah surut. Ketika Rahwana datang menyamar sebagai pertapa untuk menculik Sita, Lakshmana menolak untuk membuka garis suci (Lakshman Rekha) yang ia buat di sekitar pondok, dan ketika Rama memintanya mengejar seekor rakshasa yang menyamar sebagai rusa emas (Maricha), Lakshmana awalnya enggan meninggalkan Sita sendirian tetapi akhirnya menuruti perintah Rama setelah Sita mendesaknya, sebuah keputusan yang ironis justru membuka jalan bagi penculikan Sita. Dalam pertempuran di Lanka, Lakshmana bertempur sebagai perwira utama pasukan Rama, menghadapi Indrajit (Meghnad), putra Rahwana yang sangat sakti, dalam pertempuran dahsyat yang hingga menewaskan Indrajit, meskipun Lakshmana sendiri terluka parah oleh mantra Nagapasha (rantai ular) Indrajit dan harus ditolong oleh obat suci dari Hanuman yang membawa gunung Dronagiri.

Kisah Lakshmana yang paling menyentuh hati terjadi ketika Rama, setelah Sita dikembalikan dari tawanan, mengasingkan Sita karena keraguan rakyat Ayodhya atas kesuciannya. Lakshmana, yang sangat menyayangi Sita sebagai kakak ipar, harus menjadi pelaksana pengasingan tersebut — sebuah tugas yang menghancurkan hatinya karena ia harus menuruti perintah Rama sambil melihat penderitaan Sita yang tak bersalah. Setelah kematian Rama, Lakshmana diperintahkan untuk menemani Bharata menjaga sandal Rama sebagai simbol pemerintahan, dan pada akhirnya, ketika Lakshmana mencapai batas umurnya, ia memilih untuk melepaskan nyawanya di Sungai Sarayu, memasuki Vaikuntha (surga Wisnu) dan menyatu kembali dengan Shesha Naga, menutup kisah kesetiaan yang menjadi standar tertinggi hubungan persaudaraan dalam tradisi Hindu. ''',
    storyEn: '''
Lakshmana is the son of King Dasharatha of Ayodhya and Queen Sumitra, and twin brother of Shatrughna. Spiritually he is regarded as an incarnation of Shesha Naga, the cosmic serpent upon whom Vishnu reclines, reflecting the inseparable bond between Lakshmana and Rama — wherever Vishnu (Rama) is present, there too will Shesha (Lakshmana) be found. From childhood Lakshmana was deeply devoted to Rama, honoring his brother even more than his own father, and when Rama was exiled to the forest for fourteen years, Lakshmana resolved to accompany him without the slightest hesitation, leaving behind his own wife Urmila in Ayodhya.

During the exile Lakshmana served as Rama and Sita's primary protector, guarding their forest hermitage with vigilance that never waned. When Ravana came disguised as an ascetic to abduct Sita, Lakshmana had drawn a protective line (Lakshman Rekha) around their dwelling and refused to open it, and when Rama asked him to chase a rakshasa disguised as a golden deer (Maricha), Lakshmana was reluctant to leave Sita alone but ultimately complied with Rama's command after Sita urged him — a decision that ironically opened the path for Sita's abduction. In the war at Lanka Lakshmana fought as Rama's chief lieutenant, confronting Indrajit (Meghnad), Ravana's supremely powerful son, in a titanic battle that ended in Indrajit's death, although Lakshmana himself was gravely wounded by Indrajit's Nagapasha (serpent chain) spell and had to be saved by a miraculous herb brought by Hanuman from the mountain Dronagiri.

Lakshmana's most poignant episode came after the war, when Rama, plagued by the doubts of Ayodhya's citizens regarding Sita's purity, ordered her exile into the forest. Lakshmana, who loved Sita dearly as his sister-in-law, was forced to be the executor of that exile — a task that shattered his heart as he had to obey Rama's command while witnessing the suffering of an innocent Sita. After Rama's death, Lakshmana was instructed to accompany Bharata in safeguarding Rama's sandals as a symbol of rulership, and at the end of his life, when his time had come, Lakshmana chose to release his life into the river Sarayu, entering Vaikuntha (Vishnu's heaven) and reuniting with Shesha Naga, closing a story of loyalty that became the highest standard of fraternal devotion in Hindu tradition. ''',
